WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.536
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level one.

2
00:00:05.836 --> 00:00:09.466
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.766 --> 00:00:16.238
Unit sixteen: Fact-Checking News. Today you will make contrasts with although, while and however.

4
00:00:16.538 --> 00:00:19.225
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:23.434
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.283 --> 00:00:25.447
Fact-checker.

7
00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:30.121
Fact-checker means a person who checks whether claims are true.

8
00:00:30.717 --> 00:00:32.797
The fact-checker found three errors.

9
00:00:33.383 --> 00:00:35.978
Your turn. Say: Fact-checker.

10
00:00:38.833 --> 00:00:39.962
Fact-checker.

11
00:00:40.717 --> 00:00:42.147
Claim verification.

12
00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:47.146
Claim verification means the process of testing whether a statement is true.

13
00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:50.376
Claim verification uses several sources.

14
00:00:50.983 --> 00:00:54.158
Your turn. Say: Claim verification.

15
00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.326
Claim verification.

16
00:00:59.083 --> 00:01:00.363
Misinformation.

17
00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:05.741
Misinformation means false information spread, often without bad intent.

18
00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:09.127
Misinformation spreads faster than facts.

19
00:01:09.717 --> 00:01:12.346
Your turn. Say: Misinformation.

20
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:16.410
Misinformation.

21
00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:18.499
Primary source.

22
00:01:19.483 --> 00:01:23.213
Primary source means an original document or witness of an event.

23
00:01:23.817 --> 00:01:26.373
A primary source is the best evidence.

24
00:01:26.983 --> 00:01:29.752
Your turn. Say: Primary source.

25
00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:34.019
Primary source.

26
00:01:34.783 --> 00:01:35.935
Rating system.

27
00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:40.296
Rating system means a scale showing how true a claim is.

28
00:01:40.883 --> 00:01:43.649
The rating system used true, mixed and false.

29
00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:47.100
Your turn. Say: Rating system.

30
00:01:49.933 --> 00:01:51.410
Rating system.

31
00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:53.139
Context.

32
00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:57.531
Context means the situation that helps explain a statement.

33
00:01:58.117 --> 00:02:00.673
Without context, the quote is misleading.

34
00:02:01.283 --> 00:02:03.947
Your turn. Say: Context.

35
00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:07.906
Context.

36
00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:09.489
Debunk.

37
00:02:10.483 --> 00:02:13.783
Debunk means to show that a belief or story is false.

38
00:02:14.383 --> 00:02:16.975
The team debunked the viral rumour.

39
00:02:17.583 --> 00:02:19.922
Your turn. Say: Debunk.

40
00:02:22.767 --> 00:02:23.663
Debunk.

41
00:02:24.417 --> 00:02:25.604
Accuracy.

42
00:02:26.617 --> 00:02:29.777
Accuracy means being correct and free from mistakes.

43
00:02:30.383 --> 00:02:32.801
Accuracy is the heart of journalism.

44
00:02:33.417 --> 00:02:35.988
Your turn. Say: Accuracy.

45
00:02:38.833 --> 00:02:39.985
Accuracy.

46
00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:44.567
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:45.417 --> 00:02:51.018
Visal, a post says unemployment is falling, while the fact-checker rates it 'mixed'. Why?

48
00:02:52.117 --> 00:03:00.377
Although the figure is correct, it ignores context. Fact-checkers contrast what is true with what is missing.

49
00:03:01.483 --> 00:03:03.587
Which words show contrast?

50
00:03:04.683 --> 00:03:12.316
Although and while begin a clause: 'Although the number fell, many jobs are part-time.' However begins a new sentence.

51
00:03:13.417 --> 00:03:15.277
And how do they verify a claim?

52
00:03:16.383 --> 00:03:24.176
They find a primary source, compare other evidence, add context, and then give the claim a rating.

53
00:03:25.333 --> 00:03:26.996
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:31.429
Grammar focus: Contrast with although and while and however.

55
00:03:31.729 --> 00:03:32.939
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:37.182
Although the figure is correct, it ignores important context.

57
00:03:38.083 --> 00:03:41.279
While the post is popular, it contains misinformation.

58
00:03:42.183 --> 00:03:46.786
The claim sounds convincing. However, no primary source supports it.

59
00:03:47.733 --> 00:03:55.299
Although and while join two clauses with a comma. However starts a new sentence and is followed by a comma. Never use 'but' with although.

60
00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:59.921
Be careful! Don't say: Although true, but it misleads.

61
00:04:01.867 --> 00:04:04.632
Say: Although it is true, it misleads.

62
00:04:04.932 --> 00:04:06.595
Do not use 'but' with although.

63
00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:10.076
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:10.867 --> 00:04:12.332
How Facts Get Checked.

65
00:04:12.632 --> 00:04:15.793
A fact-checker tests whether a popular claim is true.

66
00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:22.631
Although a viral post may sound convincing, claim verification begins with a primary source.

67
00:04:23.633 --> 00:04:27.653
The fact-checker then adds context, because a true number can still mislead.

68
00:04:28.667 --> 00:04:33.011
While the rating system is simple, the research behind each rating takes hours.

69
00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:36.998
However, every debunked rumour protects accuracy.

70
00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:41.858
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:42.717 --> 00:04:45.645
blank the figure is correct, it ignores context.

72
00:04:45.945 --> 00:04:49.506
A, Although. B, However. C, Because of.

73
00:04:53.067 --> 00:04:54.939
The answer is A: Although.

74
00:04:56.083 --> 00:04:59.639
False information spread, often without bad intent, is blank.

75
00:04:59.939 --> 00:05:03.975
A, misinformation. B, accuracy. C, a rating.

76
00:05:07.533 --> 00:05:09.661
The answer is A: misinformation.

77
00:05:10.817 --> 00:05:13.176
To show that a rumour is false is to blank it.

78
00:05:13.476 --> 00:05:17.106
A, debunk. B, verify. C, publish.

79
00:05:20.667 --> 00:05:22.840
The answer is A: debunk.

80
00:05:23.983 --> 00:05:25.716
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:26.016 --> 00:05:33.977
A, Although it is true, but it misleads.. B, Although it is true, it misleads.. C, Although it true, it misleads..

82
00:05:37.533 --> 00:05:40.694
The answer is B: Although it is true, it misleads..

83
00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:45.208
According to the reading, claim verification begins with a…

84
00:05:45.508 --> 00:05:49.150
A, rumour. B, primary source. C, rating.

85
00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:55.326
The answer is B: primary source.

86
00:05:56.533 --> 00:05:59.859
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:00.717 --> 00:06:04.539
What steps could you take yourself to fact-check a claim you see online?

88
00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:14.920
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:37.893
First, I would find the original source of the claim, not only the post that shares it. Next, I would search for the same information on trusted news sites and official websites. Then I would check the date and context, because old facts can be used wrongly. Finally, I would ask a teacher if I am still unsure.

90
00:06:38.193 --> 00:06:40.100
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:40.983 --> 00:06:45.842
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and contrast with although and while and however.

92
00:06:46.142 --> 00:06:51.963
Today's value is intellectual honesty. Follow the evidence, even when it is inconvenient.

93
00:06:52.263 --> 00:06:55.055
See you in Unit seventeen. Goodbye!

94
00:06:55.355 --> 00:06:58.193
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

